// ============================================================================
== 2.1 The Shape of the Story

The novel covers one term in Bepo's life, and inside that term it carries twenty-four
years. The trick of the structure is that the present is thin and slow — a man waiting to
leave — while the past arrives in solid blocks, each one an argument for staying.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch02_three_movements.svg",
  "Three movements. The first announces the decision, the second shows what it costs, the third reverses it.", width: 100%)

#concept-box("Why the middle is the argument")[
  Chapters 5 to 8 are not a subplot. Each one puts a piece of Bepo's working life in front of the reader — the money, the teaching, the corruption he escaped, the work he has not finished — and each piece makes the departure more expensive.

  The novel could have told the same story in five chapters. It spends four on the school because the question it is really asking is not *should he go* but *what is he leaving*.
]

#rule-box("The three movements, and what each one is for")[
  + *Pressure, Chapters 1–4.* The decision is announced before the reader knows what it costs: the breakdown, the admission, the tales from abroad, and a family destroyed by a visa.
  + *The school, Chapters 5–8.* Four episodes from the working year: the cooperative, the grammar row, the ritual he escaped, and the missions he cannot finish.
  + *The decision, Chapters 9–12.* Heritage, passport, farewell, flight. The past is put physically in front of him at Badagry, and the last chapter reverses the first.
]

// ============================================================================
== 2.2 Chapters 1–4: The Pressure

*Chapter 1 — Dusk.* At morning assembly Bepo takes the microphone, tries to speak, and
breaks down. He drops the microphone and is heard to say *Save me, O God!* The school
nurse, Mrs Titi, offers a handkerchief; the vice principal, Mrs Grace Apeh, takes charge
and asks the chemistry teacher, Mr Justus Anabel, to dismiss the students; the principal
is walked to his office. The managing director, Mrs Ibidun Gloss, is sent for and arrives
at half past nine. She calls Mr Ope Wande, the physics teacher who is also a pastor, but
ten minutes of gentle questioning gets nothing out of him. By afternoon the parents are
ringing the school to ask what has happened.

*Chapter 2 — The Enticement.* Five days later the reason comes out: Bepo is leaving for
the United Kingdom. His wife Seri, a nurse, is already there with their two children, Nike
and Kike, and the marriage has been strained by his foot-dragging. The figures are put on
the table by his colleagues — Seri earns about £10,000 a month, the teaching post waiting
for him pays about £3,600, and his salary at Stardom is ₦400,000 a month. He has been at
Stardom for twenty-four years, from headmaster of Stardom Kiddies to principal of the
secondary school, and he still dreams of starting a school of his own in Nigeria. Mr Audu
and Pastor Ope Wande think the move is a golden opportunity. Bepo does not argue with
them; he simply cannot make the announcement sound like good news.

*Chapter 3 — Migration Tales.* The staff trade stories the way people do when someone is
about to do what they only talk about. Sola, a former colleague now in the United Kingdom,
says the transition will be easy because Seri is settled. Others tell the other half: Jare,
a banker, ended up caring for an elderly couple in London and wept when he understood what
the job was; Hope, an accountant who went to do a master's degree, was supported by his
wife until she stopped paying. The chapter also measures the drain — about a thousand of
Nigeria's three thousand doctors leave each year — and the desperate ways people fund the
ticket, including a member of staff who took a two-million-naira cooperative loan and
disappeared abroad, and a driver who tried to sell the school's bus.

*Chapter 4 — A Case of Visa Denied.* A late-night call from Mrs Ignatius, a parent, brings
the theme home. The family had been preparing to relocate: her husband, a manager in an
oil firm, had closed his clearing and forwarding business; she had learned tailoring and
hairdressing to earn abroad. Then the medical examination for the visa produced a DNA
result, and Mr Ignatius learned that he was not the father of Favour, their daughter in
SSS2 at Stardom. He ordered mother and daughter out of the house. Bepo listens, sympathises,
and can do nothing — which is the chapter's point about the limits of a headmaster's
authority.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch02_twelve.svg",
  "The twelve chapters in one line each. Learn the titles in order, then hang one fact on each title.", width: 100%)

#trap-box("Chapter 4 is not about Favour")[
  The DNA result is the plot, but the chapter is about a family that had already sold its furniture when the visa failed. The question the examiner builds from it is about the *cost of migration*, not about the paternity test — and an option that describes only the scandal is the half-truth.
]

// ============================================================================
== 2.3 Chapters 5–8: The School

*Chapter 5 — Snake in the Roof.* Mrs Gloss notices staff cars parked on a piece of school
land and suspects that the school's money is being taken. The security guard's report leads
to the cooperative society, which has built a purse of about ₦95 million and used it to
help teachers buy cars. At the board meeting, the chairman — Chief Mrs Solape Bayo, the
MD's mother — says that a staff this independent is like a snake hung in the roof: you go
to bed under it. The board caps loans at ₦250,000. The chapter is about money, class and
fear, and it is the clearest picture in the novel of how a school is actually run.

*Chapter 6 — Ade as Well as Jide COMES vs. COME.* At Open Day a parent, Mr Guta, produces
his son's note and demands the English teacher's head: the note reads *Ade as well as Jide
comes early*, and the parent is certain the verb is wrong. Mrs Gloss reads the note, agrees
with the parent, and orders Mr Fafore's dismissal. Bepo explains the rule — where two
subjects are joined by *as well as*, the verb agrees with the first — and the MD laughs it
off. Mr Audu ends the scene with a joke. Fafore, who earns ₦175,000 a month, lives in Ifo
and wakes at four to beat the traffic, has twice been the school's most punctual teacher.

*Chapter 7 — Ritualists.* The chapter that explains the twenty-four years. Bepo remembers
Beesway Group of Schools, where the director, Egi Meko, would not be corrected about a
mistake in the school's own name and where a parent once asked Bepo to perform a rite that
would flood the school with pupils. It ends with the night Bepo saw men in the distance,
took a machete, and found the director among them, a cow being buried alive. Bepo protested,
was struck on the head and fell, and left the school. The chapter folds in a second memory:
Fruitful Future, the small school he co-founded, which grew to more than seventy pupils and
then failed when the roads went bad and the fee-paying families moved away.

*Chapter 8 — Missions Unaccomplished.* What Bepo has not finished. Two SSS2 students,
Banky and Tosh, have been at war since JSS3, and their parents are now in court: Banky
called Tosh the son of an ex-convict, and Tosh's father, Chief Didi Ogba — detained for
thirty-six months over ₦2.5 billion before being released and ordered to refund — wants a
written apology published in the school magazine. The prefect election, with forms at
₦50,000 for head boy and head girl, ₦40,000 for deputies and ₦25,000 for the rest, ends
with Tosh walking out. And the Invention Club is building phones out of recycled material,
a project Bepo is patron of and will not see finished.

#shortcut-box("The four school chapters, in one word each")[
  + Chapter 5: *money.*
  + Chapter 6: *teaching.*
  + Chapter 7: *corruption.*
  + Chapter 8: *unfinished work.*

  If a question asks which of these the novel is illustrating in a given scene, the one-word list settles it before you re-read a line.
]

// ============================================================================
== 2.4 Chapters 9–12: The Decision

*Chapter 9 — Laughing Waterfalls.* Bepo's excursion programme takes Stardom students across
the country: the waterfalls at Ikogosi, Erin-Ijesha, Owu and Gurara, festivals from Calabar
to Argungu, the palaces and museums of the north and west. In Lagos they visit the high
places and the low ones, and when the students laugh at Mushin, Ajegunle and Ijora, Bepo
tells them that being born in a place like that condemns nobody to a life of poverty. The
chapter ends in Badagry, at the Akran's palace, the First Storey Building and the Black
Heritage Museum, and at the Point of No Return. Standing where people were shipped,
Bepo cannot stop hearing the rhyme between the slave ship and the plane.

*Chapter 10 — Passport Pains.* An expired passport forces Bepo into the machinery of the
Nigerian state: the immigration office in Ibadan, the NIN desk, network failures, queues,
agents and a payment of ₦100,000 to move things along. He notices the new expressway and
the churches and mosques going up beside it, and reflects on a country that is more
religious and less patient than it was. The chapter is comic and it is the novel's least
romantic account of patriotism: he is leaving because the queue took a week.

*Chapter 11 — Point of No Return.* The school gives him a farewell: a novelty football
match in which staff and students play together, a debate on arts against sciences, songs,
comedy, dances, and a speech from Mrs Gloss, who says that if it were hers to decide he
would never leave, and who reveals that it was her late father who hired him twenty-four
years earlier. The staff pay the airline's hundred-dollar fee when the celebration pushes
his flight back. Bepo remembers a Yoruba saying about the carver: when the carver leaves,
the carvings remain. On the last afternoon the drama club performs a dance about the
Badagry visit, and he shouts *Noooo!* in the middle of it, then covers the moment with a
joke about the canoe.

*Chapter 12 — …Dawn.* Saturday. The flight is at ten in the evening, boarding at five, and
he means to leave home at three. His landlord, Mr Ogunwale, and his family come to say
goodbye and Ogunwale drives him to the airport in his Honda Pilot. Bepo has sold his car
to the school's accountant, given his electronics to his landlady, and packed two large bags
and a small one, including the iru, egusi, crayfish and dried snail his wife asked for,
bought at Oyingbo market. Waiting to board, he falls asleep and dreams that he is at the
Heritage Museum in Badagry, counting seven million enslaved Africans being driven onto a
ship at the Point of No Return; a white man orders him aboard, and he screams *Noooo!*
An airline official wakes him: it is time to board. He does not.

On Monday the school assembles without him. Mrs Gloss tells the students that a new
principal will be appointed. Then someone sees him at the gate, and the assembly breaks
into the school's victory song. He says he is not leaving; he has come back to complete his
mission. His voice is lost in the noise.

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch02_timeline.svg",
  "The six beats of the present-tense storyline. Every sequence question is answered from one of these six, or from the chapter that contains it.", width: 100%)

// ============================================================================
== 2.5 Sequence, Flashback and the Question That Turns on One Word

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch02_flashback.svg",
  "Five flashbacks, and the work each one is doing. A flashback question asks what the memory explains, not what happened in it.", width: 100%)

#diagram-fig("diagrams/ch02_sequence.svg",
  "Four moves that settle a sequence question, in the order you should use them.", width: 80%)

#rule-box("The sequence method, in five steps")[
  + *Read the operative word.* *Before*, *after*, *immediately*, *first* and *last* each move the answer by one place.
  + *Anchor.* Put the one event you are sure of at the front or the back of the list.
  + *Place the chapter, then the scene.* Chapters are easier to order than moments.
  + *Sort the tellings from the memories.* If the question says *narrated*, the flashbacks come where they are told.
  + *Check the pair you are least sure of.* Most sequence questions reduce to one comparison.
]

#worked-example(
  1,
  "The five days",
  [How long after the morning assembly did Bepo admit that he was leaving the country?

    A. The same day \
    B. Five days \
    C. Two weeks \
    D. A month],
  [
    *Step 1 — the tell.* *How long after* is a sequence-and-fact hybrid, and the number is in the text.

    *Step 2 — the scene.* Chapter 1 ends with the school puzzled; Chapter 2 opens five days later, when the reason finally comes out.

    *Step 3 — the traps.* A is the assembly itself, where nobody learns anything — the commonest wrong answer in the book. C and D are invented intervals that would break the structure: the novel's present is thin, and five days is enough time for the MD to have asked three times.

    *Check.* If the interval were two weeks, the staff would have stopped asking. The questions keep coming because it is days, not weeks. The answer is *B*.
  ],
  tier: "FOUNDATION",
)

#worked-example(
  2,
  "The chapter that is a memory",
  [The ritual at Beesway Group of Schools is described in

    A. Chapter 5, as part of the board meeting \
    B. Chapter 7, as a memory of an earlier school \
    C. Chapter 9, during the Badagry excursion \
    D. Chapter 12, in Bepo's dream at the airport],
  [
    *Step 1 — the event.* A cow buried alive, men in the dark, a director among them: the most violent scene in the book.

    *Step 2 — the place.* It belongs to Chapter 7, *Ritualists*, and it is told as a flashback to his time at Beesway, years before the term the novel covers.

    *Step 3 — the near misses.* D is tempting because Bepo does dream at the airport — but he dreams of the slave ship at Badagry, not of Beesway. C is where Badagry is, and A is where the money is.

    *Check.* A memory is narrated in the chapter that needs it, not in the chapter when it happened. The book needs the ritual at Chapter 7 to explain why he left Beesway. The answer is *B*.
  ],
  tier: "FOUNDATION",
)

#worked-example(
  3,
  "Immediately before",
  [What happens immediately after Bepo breaks down at the assembly?

    A. The managing director arrives at half past nine. \
    B. The vice principal asks a teacher to dismiss the students. \
    C. Bepo explains that he is travelling to the United Kingdom. \
    D. The staff hold a farewell party for him.],
  [
    *Step 1 — the word.* *Immediately* is the whole question.

    *Step 2 — the scene.* Mrs Grace Apeh takes charge on the spot and asks Mr Justus Anabel to dismiss the students, while the principal is walked to his office.

    *Step 3 — the order.* The MD's arrival at 9:30 comes after that; the explanation comes five days later in Chapter 2; the farewell is Chapter 11. Three true events, all in the wrong place.

    *Check.* Read the options against the clock of the scene, not the clock of the book. The answer is *B*.
  ],
  tier: "UTME STANDARD",
)

#worked-example(
  4,
  "Arrange these events",
  [Arrange the following in the order they are narrated. (i) The board caps the cooperative's loans. (ii) Bepo renews his passport. (iii) The school holds a farewell for Bepo. (iv) Bepo tells the staff he is leaving.

    A. (iv), (i), (ii), (iii) \
    B. (i), (iv), (ii), (iii) \
    C. (iv), (ii), (i), (iii) \
    D. (ii), (iv), (i), (iii)],
  [
    *Step 1 — anchor.* (iv) is the earliest: the admission comes in Chapter 2. Every option except B starts with it, so B is out.

    *Step 2 — place the middle two.* The cooperative is Chapter 5; the passport is Chapter 10. So (iv), (i), (ii).

    *Step 3 — the end.* The farewell is Chapter 11, after the passport. The order is (iv), (i), (ii), (iii).

    *Check.* C reverses the middle pair, which is the one comparison the question is really testing. The answer is *A*.
  ],
  tier: "UTME STANDARD",
)

#worked-example(
  5,
  "The last thing that happens",
  [Which of the following is the last event in the novel?

    A. Bepo's flight is called and he walks to the gate. \
    B. The school holds a farewell debate on arts and sciences. \
    C. Bepo walks back into the school and is carried by the students. \
    D. Bepo dreams of the Point of No Return at Badagry.],
  [
    *Step 1 — the end of the book.* Chapter 12 runs Saturday at the airport into Monday morning at the school.

    *Step 2 — order the four.* The debate is Chapter 11; the dream is at the airport on Saturday night; the flight being called is the moment after the dream; the return is Monday.

    *Step 3 — the trap in A.* It is the most natural ending for a novel about migration, and the novel deliberately refuses it. A is what Bepo does not do.

    *Check.* The last paragraph of the book is an assembly breaking into a song. The answer is *C*.
  ],
  tier: "JAMB CHALLENGE",
)

